export const E6={title:'Bleeding control, shock and soft-tissue trauma (Skill V)',hook:'Uncontrolled bleeding is the leading preventable cause of death after injury. EMTs must stop external bleeding fast, recognize the early signs of shock before blood pressure falls and manage chest, abdominal and soft-tissue wounds. Skill V tests the escalation from direct pressure to a tourniquet and then shock management.',objectives:['Control external bleeding following HOSA Skill V.','Recognize compensated and decompensated shock and treat hypoperfusion.','Manage open chest wounds, abdominal injuries and impaled objects.','Assess and treat burns at the EMT level.'],sections:[
['Bleeding control: Skill V steps 1–4',[
'**1.** Verify scene safety and use BSI. **2.** Apply **direct pressure** to the wound. The judge states the wound continues to bleed. **3.** Apply a **pressure dressing**. The judge states bleeding continues. **4.** Apply a **tourniquet**: **a.** place it **proximal** to the injury—above the elbow or joint related to the injury but **not directly on the joint**; **b.** pull the free end through the buckle and tighten over the pad; **c.** engage the tightening mechanism **until distal pulses are no longer palpable and bleeding is controlled**. Note the time.',
'For junctional wounds (groin, armpit, neck) where a tourniquet cannot be placed, **pack the wound** with hemostatic or plain gauze and hold firm pressure for at least 3 minutes. Arterial bleeding spurts bright red; venous bleeding flows dark red; capillary bleeding oozes. **Internal bleeding** shows as bruising, a rigid or distended abdomen, vomiting or coughing blood, dark tarry stools and signs of shock—EMTs treat it with shock care and rapid transport to surgery.',
order('Skill V escalation',['Scene safety and BSI','Direct pressure','Pressure dressing','Tourniquet proximal to the wound, not on a joint','Tighten until bleeding stops and distal pulses are absent','Note the time'])]],
['Shock (hypoperfusion): Skill V steps 5–9',[
'**Shock** is inadequate delivery of oxygen to the tissues. **Compensated shock**: the body maintains blood pressure by raising heart rate and constricting vessels—**anxiety or restlessness**, **tachycardia**, **pale, cool, clammy skin**, **delayed capillary refill**, thirst and nausea. **Decompensated shock**: compensation fails—**falling blood pressure**, altered mental status and weak or absent peripheral pulses. **Hypotension is a late sign**; children compensate well and then crash suddenly.',
'Types include **hypovolemic** (blood or fluid loss), **cardiogenic** (pump failure), **distributive** (vessel dilation: **septic**, **anaphylactic**, **neurogenic**) and **obstructive** (tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, pulmonary embolism). The judge says bleeding is controlled and the patient shows signs of hypoperfusion: **5.** comfort, calm and reassure; **6.** position **supine**; **7.** give **high-concentration oxygen**; **8.** prevent heat loss with **blankets under and over** the patient; **9.** indicate the need for **immediate transport**.',
sort('Compensated or decompensated shock?',['Compensated','Decompensated'],[['Anxiety, tachycardia, normal blood pressure',0],['Falling blood pressure and confusion',1],['Pale, cool skin with delayed capillary refill',0],['Absent radial pulse',1],['Thirst and restlessness',0]])]],
['Chest injuries',[
'An **open (sucking) chest wound** lets air enter the chest cavity; cover it immediately with an **occlusive dressing** (vented chest seal or a dressing taped on three sides) during the primary survey. Watch for **tension pneumothorax**: increasing shortness of breath, absent breath sounds on one side, JVD, hypotension and, late, tracheal deviation—if a seal is in place, “burp” it by lifting one edge and call for ALS.',
'A **flail chest**—two or more adjacent ribs broken in two or more places—causes **paradoxical motion**; support ventilation with a BVM and give oxygen. **Cardiac tamponade** (blood around the heart) produces **Beck’s triad**: JVD, muffled heart sounds and narrowing pulse pressure with hypotension. **Rib fractures** cause pain on breathing; avoid taping the chest tightly.',
match('Chest injury and finding',[['Sucking chest wound','Occlusive dressing'],['Tension pneumothorax','Absent breath sounds, JVD, hypotension'],['Flail chest','Paradoxical chest motion'],['Cardiac tamponade','JVD, muffled heart sounds, hypotension'],['Rib fracture','Pain with breathing'],['Hemothorax','Blood in the chest cavity']])]],
['Abdominal and soft-tissue injuries',[
'Abdominal organs are **solid** (liver, spleen, kidneys, pancreas—bleed heavily) or **hollow** (stomach, intestines, bladder—spill contents and cause peritonitis). Signs include pain, guarding, rigidity, distension and shock. An **evisceration** (organs protruding through a wound) is covered with a **moist, sterile dressing** and then an **occlusive dressing** to keep it warm—**never push organs back in**. Position the patient supine with the knees bent if no spinal injury.',
'Soft-tissue injuries are **closed** (contusions, hematomas, crush injuries) or **open** (abrasions, lacerations, avulsions, punctures, amputations). **Impaled objects** are stabilized in place with bulky dressings—remove only if they block the airway or interfere with CPR, such as an object through the cheek. **Amputated parts** are wrapped in sterile gauze, sealed in a bag and kept cool on ice water. Penetrating neck wounds need an occlusive dressing to prevent an air embolism.',
match('Wound and care',[['Evisceration','Moist sterile then occlusive dressing'],['Impaled object in the thigh','Stabilize in place'],['Object through the cheek blocking the airway','May be removed'],['Amputated finger','Wrap, bag and cool'],['Open neck wound','Occlusive dressing'],['Avulsion','Replace skin flap and dress']])]],
['Burns at the EMT level',[
'Stop the burning, remove jewelry and non-adhered clothing and cool **superficial and small partial-thickness burns** with cool water. Estimate the **total body surface area (TBSA)** with the **rule of nines** (adult head 9%, each arm 9%, front trunk 18%, back 18%, each leg 18%, genitals 1%) or the **palm (about 1%)**. Cover large burns with **dry, sterile dressings** and keep the patient **warm**—large burns lose heat fast. Give oxygen, watch the airway (singed nasal hair, soot, hoarseness) and transport to a **burn center** when indicated.',
'**Critical burns** include full-thickness burns over 10% TBSA, partial-thickness over 30%, burns with respiratory injury, burns of the face, hands, feet or genitals, **circumferential** burns and burns with other trauma. **Chemical burns**: brush off powders and flush with large amounts of water. **Electrical burns**: ensure the power is off, look for entry and exit wounds and monitor for cardiac arrest.',
{t:'w',kind:'burns'}]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Forearm laceration',{a:{text:'A patient has spurting bleeding from the forearm after a saw accident. (Scenario invented for practice.) Direct pressure and a pressure dressing fail. What next?',choices:[['Apply a tourniquet proximal to the wound, not over the elbow, and tighten until bleeding stops and the radial pulse is gone','b','Correct.',true],['Remove the dressing to look','b','Do not remove dressings.',false]]},b:{text:'Bleeding is controlled. The patient is pale, cool and restless with a pulse of 118. What do you do?',choices:[['Supine, high-concentration oxygen, blankets under and over and immediate transport','c','Correct. Shock care.',true],['Sit the patient up and offer water','c','No fluids by mouth; supine.',false]]},c:{text:'You note the tourniquet time and reassess every 5 minutes en route.',end:true}})]],
["Skill V tips and documentation",["In Skill V, the judge escalates each step: direct pressure fails, then the pressure dressing fails, then the tourniquet is required. Say each step aloud—“Applying direct pressure,” “Placing a pressure dressing,” “Applying tourniquet proximal to the wound”—and **verbalize checking the distal pulse** after tightening. Tell the patient-actor the tourniquet will be painful and **record the time** of application on the tourniquet and in the report.","Once bleeding is controlled, move quickly into shock care: **calm and reassure**, **supine position**, **high-concentration oxygen** by non-rebreather, **blankets under and over** and **immediate transport**. These four shock steps are separate points on the rating sheet, so do not combine them silently.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Skill V scoring sequence","steps":["Scene safety and BSI","Direct pressure","Pressure dressing","Tourniquet and distal pulse check","Reassure and place supine","High-concentration oxygen and blankets","Immediate transport"]}]]
],takeaways:['Skill V: direct pressure, pressure dressing, then tourniquet proximal to the wound but not on a joint, tightened until bleeding stops and distal pulses are absent; pack junctional wounds; note the time.','Compensated shock: anxiety, tachycardia, pale cool clammy skin, delayed capillary refill; hypotension is late; treat with supine position, high-concentration oxygen, blankets under and over and immediate transport.','Occlusive dressing for sucking chest wounds (watch for tension pneumothorax); moist sterile then occlusive dressing for eviscerations; stabilize impaled objects; rule of nines and dry dressings for large burns.'],terms:[['Hypoperfusion','Shock—inadequate delivery of oxygen to tissues.'],['Occlusive dressing','An airtight dressing used for open chest and neck wounds.'],['Evisceration','Abdominal organs protruding through a wound.']],mcqs:[q('Per Skill V, a tourniquet is tightened until:','Bleeding is controlled and distal pulses are no longer palpable','The patient stops talking','The skin turns red','It is comfortable','Note the time.'),q('Where is the tourniquet placed per Skill V?','Proximal to the injury, not directly on the joint','Directly on the elbow','Distal to the wound','On the wound','Above the wound.'),q('Which is a late sign of shock?','Falling blood pressure','Anxiety','Tachycardia','Pale skin','Decompensated.'),q('Per Skill V, a patient with hypoperfusion is positioned:','Supine','Sitting','Prone','Standing','Plus oxygen and blankets.'),q('An open chest wound is covered with:','An occlusive dressing','A wet towel','Nothing','A tourniquet','Vented chest seal.'),q('How is an evisceration covered?','Moist sterile dressing, then occlusive dressing','Push the organs back in','Dry gauze only','Ice packs','Never push back.'),q('Paradoxical chest movement indicates:','Flail chest','Asthma','Cardiac arrest','Normal breathing','Two or more ribs in two places.'),q('Using the rule of nines, an adult’s entire front trunk is:','18%','9%','1%','36%','Back is also 18%.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how an EMT controls severe extremity bleeding and manages shock following HOSA Skill V, and explain the signs that distinguish compensated from decompensated shock.',rubric:[['Bleeding control',['direct pressure','pressure dressing','tourniquet','proximal','not','joint','distal pulses','time']],['Shock care',['comfort','supine','high-concentration oxygen','blankets','under and over','immediate transport']],['Compensated signs',['anxiety','restless','tachycardia','pale','cool','clammy','capillary refill']],['Decompensated signs',['blood pressure','falling','hypotension','late','altered','absent','radial']]],model:'After verifying scene safety and taking BSI precautions, I apply firm direct pressure to the wound. If it keeps bleeding, I apply a pressure dressing, and if bleeding continues despite that, I apply a tourniquet proximal to the injury—above the wound and the related joint but not directly on the joint—pull the free end through the buckle, tighten it over the pad and turn the windlass until the bleeding is controlled and the distal pulse is no longer palpable, then note the time. When the patient shows signs of hypoperfusion, I comfort, calm and reassure them, place them supine, give high-concentration oxygen by non-rebreather, put blankets under and over them to prevent heat loss and call for immediate transport. Early or compensated shock appears while the blood pressure is still normal: the patient is anxious or restless, has a rapid pulse, pale, cool, clammy skin, delayed capillary refill, thirst and nausea, because the body is speeding up the heart and constricting vessels. Decompensated shock occurs when these mechanisms fail: the blood pressure falls, the mental status worsens and peripheral pulses become weak or absent. Because hypotension is a late sign, I treat shock based on the early signs and reassess every 5 minutes.'},sources:[RULES,STOP_BLEED,MEDLINE_SHOCK]};

export const E7={title:'Musculoskeletal, head and spine injuries (Skill IV)',hook:'A broken wrist is painful but rarely deadly; a broken femur can hide a liter of blood; a missed spinal injury can paralyze. EMTs assess every injured limb for circulation, sensation and movement, immobilize carefully and protect the brain and spine. Skill IV tests long bone or joint immobilization.',objectives:['Describe fractures, dislocations, sprains and strains, including named fractures.','Immobilize a long bone or joint following HOSA Skill IV.','Recognize traumatic brain injury and increased intracranial pressure.','Apply spinal motion restriction.'],sections:[
['Musculoskeletal injuries',[
'A **fracture** may be **closed** or **open**, **displaced** or **nondisplaced**. Named fractures you should know include **Colles’ fracture** (distal radius, often from a fall on an outstretched hand—“dinner fork” deformity), **greenstick** (incomplete break in a child’s flexible bone), **comminuted** (bone broken into several pieces), **hip fracture** (proximal femur; the leg is often **shortened and externally rotated**) and **femur shaft** fractures, which can lose **1–1.5 L of blood**. **Pelvic fractures** can cause life-threatening internal bleeding.',
'A **dislocation** displaces a joint (shoulder, finger, knee, hip); a **sprain** injures ligaments; a **strain** injures muscles or tendons. **Compartment syndrome**—swelling inside a closed muscle compartment—causes pain out of proportion to the injury and requires emergency care. Signs of musculoskeletal injury follow **DCAP-BTLS**, plus guarding, crepitus and loss of function.',
match('Injury and description',[['Colles’ fracture','Fracture of the distal radius'],['Greenstick fracture','Incomplete break in a child’s bone'],['Comminuted fracture','Bone broken into several pieces'],['Hip fracture','Leg shortened and externally rotated'],['Femur fracture','Can lose 1–1.5 L of blood'],['Compartment syndrome','Pain out of proportion from swelling']])]],
['Immobilization: Skill IV steps 1–6',[
'**1.** Verify scene safety. **2.** **Remove clothing** from the area of suspected injury. **3.** Inspect for **DCAP-BTLS**: deformity, contusions, abrasions, punctures/penetrations, burns, tenderness, lacerations and swelling. **4.** Note **neurovascular status distal to the injury**—**pulse, sensation and movement** (PMS or CSM). The judge states motor, sensory and circulatory functions are present and normal. **5.** **Stabilize the bones and joints above and below** the injury. **6.** **Maintain manual stabilization** to minimize movement and support the injury site.',
'If distal pulses are **absent** in a deformed long bone, local protocol may allow gentle **in-line traction** to realign it before splinting; never force a joint. Do not push protruding bone ends back in; cover open fractures with a sterile dressing first.',
order('Skill IV steps 1–6',['Verify scene safety','Remove clothing from the injured area','Inspect for DCAP-BTLS','Check distal pulse, sensation and movement','Stabilize the bones and joints above and below','Maintain manual stabilization'])]],
['Splinting: Skill IV steps 7–12',[
'**7.** Place the splint **under or alongside** the limb. **8.** **Pad** between the limb and splint for even pressure and contact; if the arm is involved, place the **hand in the position of function** (fingers slightly curled, as if holding a ball, often with a roll of gauze in the palm). Secure with cravats or bandages. **9.** **Reassess distal motor, sensory and circulatory function**. **10–12.** Hand rub, communication with the patient and partner and BSI throughout.',
'Splint types include **rigid** (padded board, SAM splint), **formable** (vacuum, ladder), **traction splints** (for isolated **mid-shaft femur** fractures—not for hip, knee or lower-leg injuries) and the **sling and swathe** for shoulder and upper-arm injuries. A **pelvic binder** can stabilize an unstable pelvis. Apply a cold pack and elevate when possible to reduce swelling.',
order('Skill IV steps 7–9',['Place the splint under or alongside the limb','Pad for even pressure','Hand in the position of function for the arm','Secure the splint','Reassess distal motor, sensory and circulation']),
label('joint','A synovial joint',[['bone','Bone','Fractures need the joints above and below immobilized.'],['ligament','Ligament','Injured in a sprain.'],['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Cushions the bone ends.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Torn in many dislocations.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Produces fluid; swells after injury.']])]],
['Head injuries',[
'A **traumatic brain injury (TBI)** may be **mild** (concussion) or severe, with bleeding inside the skull: **epidural hematoma** (often a brief loss of consciousness, a **lucid interval**, then rapid decline) or **subdural hematoma** (slower, common in older adults and those on blood thinners). Signs include altered mental status, headache, vomiting, unequal pupils, seizures, **raccoon eyes** (bruising around the eyes) and **Battle’s sign** (bruising behind the ears)—both suggest a skull base fracture—and **clear fluid** (cerebrospinal fluid) from the nose or ears.',
'**Increased intracranial pressure** produces **Cushing’s triad**: **rising blood pressure**, **slowing pulse** and **irregular respirations**. EMT care: spinal motion restriction, airway management, oxygen to keep SpO₂ at least 94%, ventilation at the normal rate (avoid hyperventilation unless directed), elevate the head of the stretcher if no shock, monitor with the **Glasgow Coma Scale** (eye 4, verbal 5, motor 6; total 3–15) and transport to a trauma center.',
flip('Head injury signs',[['Raccoon eyes','Bruising around the eyes; skull base fracture'],['Battle’s sign','Bruising behind the ears'],['Cushing’s triad','Rising BP, slowing pulse, irregular breathing'],['Lucid interval','Epidural hematoma pattern'],['GCS range','3 to 15'],['Clear fluid from ears','Possible CSF leak']])]],
['Spinal injuries and spinal motion restriction',[
'Suspect spinal injury with significant MOI, altered mental status, intoxication, neck or back pain or tenderness, neurologic deficits (numbness, weakness, tingling) or distracting injuries. **Spinal motion restriction (SMR)** means keeping the spine in line: **manual stabilization** of the head, a **cervical collar** sized correctly, and moving the patient as a unit with a **log roll**, scoop stretcher or vacuum mattress. Long backboards are now used mainly for **extrication** rather than transport in many systems.',
'**Neurogenic shock** from a high spinal cord injury causes **hypotension with a normal or slow heart rate** and warm, dry skin below the injury, because sympathetic control is lost. Patients with spinal injuries may lose the ability to breathe if the injury is above the C5 level, so monitor ventilation closely.',
kase('Fall on an outstretched hand',{a:{text:'An older adult fell on an outstretched hand; the wrist has a “dinner fork” deformity. (Scenario invented for practice.) What injury is likely?',choices:[['Colles’ fracture of the distal radius','b','Correct.',true],['Hip fracture','b','The wrist is involved.',false]]},b:{text:'Distal pulse, sensation and movement are normal. How do you splint it?',choices:[['Pad a rigid splint under the forearm and wrist, hand in the position of function, then reassess PMS','c','Correct.',true],['Straighten the wrist forcefully first','c','Splint in position found.',false]]},c:{text:'You apply a sling, a cold pack and reassess PMS before transport.',end:true}})]],
["Specific splinting techniques",["**Shoulder and upper arm** injuries are immobilized with a **sling and swathe**. **Elbow** injuries are splinted in the position found, immobilizing the humerus and forearm. **Forearm and wrist** injuries (including Colles’ fractures) use a padded rigid splint from the elbow to the fingertips with the hand in the position of function. **Hip** fractures are immobilized on a scoop stretcher or long board with pillows, in the position found. **Knee** injuries are splinted in the position found with padded boards on both sides.","**Mid-shaft femur** fractures without hip, knee or lower-leg injury may receive a **traction splint**, which reduces pain and blood loss by pulling the thigh muscles back to length. **Lower-leg and ankle** injuries can use padded boards, vacuum splints or a pillow splint for the ankle. Check and document pulse, sensation and movement before and after every splint.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Injury and splint","pairs":[["Shoulder","Sling and swathe"],["Wrist","Padded rigid splint, hand in position of function"],["Mid-shaft femur","Traction splint"],["Hip","Scoop or long board with pillows"],["Knee","Splint in position found"],["Ankle","Pillow or vacuum splint"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Colles’ fracture is the distal radius; hip fractures leave the leg shortened and externally rotated; femur fractures can lose 1–1.5 L of blood; check DCAP-BTLS and distal PMS.','Skill IV: remove clothing, DCAP-BTLS, check distal pulse, sensation and movement, stabilize joints above and below, manual stabilization, splint under or alongside, pad, hand in position of function, reassess.','TBI: raccoon eyes, Battle’s sign, CSF leak, Cushing’s triad (rising BP, slowing pulse, irregular breathing), GCS 3–15; spinal motion restriction with manual stabilization, collar and log roll; neurogenic shock has low BP with a normal or slow pulse.'],terms:[['Colles’ fracture','A fracture of the distal radius.'],['Position of function','A relaxed hand position with fingers slightly curled.'],['Cushing’s triad','Rising blood pressure, slowing pulse and irregular respirations from increased intracranial pressure.']],mcqs:[q('What is the term for a fracture of the distal radius?','Colles’ Fracture','Rotation Fracture','Supracondylar','Tommy John','HOSA sample question.'),q('Before and after splinting, the EMT checks distal:','Pulse, sensation and movement','Blood glucose','Pupils','Temperature only','PMS or CSM.'),q('An injured hand is splinted in the:','Position of function','Fully extended position','Clenched fist','Bent backward','Fingers slightly curled.'),q('A traction splint is used for an isolated:','Mid-shaft femur fracture','Hip fracture','Ankle fracture','Wrist fracture','Not hip or knee.'),q('Bruising behind the ears is called:','Battle’s sign','Raccoon eyes','Cushing’s triad','Beck’s triad','Skull base fracture.'),q('Cushing’s triad includes:','Rising blood pressure, slowing pulse and irregular respirations','Low BP, fast pulse, fast breathing','Fever, rash and cough','JVD, muffled heart sounds and hypotension','Increased intracranial pressure.'),q('The Glasgow Coma Scale ranges from:','3 to 15','0 to 10','1 to 5','10 to 20','Eye, verbal, motor.'),q('Neurogenic shock typically causes:','Low blood pressure with a normal or slow heart rate','High blood pressure','Fast pulse with cool skin','No change','Loss of sympathetic tone.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how an EMT immobilizes a suspected forearm fracture following HOSA Skill IV, and explain how the EMT recognizes and manages a serious head injury.',rubric:[['Assessment',['scene','remove clothing','dcap-btls','pulse','sensation','movement','distal']],['Splinting',['above and below','manual stabilization','under','alongside','pad','position of function','reassess']],['Head injury signs',['altered','unequal pupils','raccoon','battle','fluid','cushing','blood pressure','pulse','respirations']],['Head injury care',['spinal motion restriction','airway','oxygen','94','gcs','trauma center','hyperventilation']]],model:'I make sure the scene is safe, use BSI and remove clothing from the injured forearm so I can inspect it for DCAP-BTLS—deformity, contusions, abrasions, punctures, burns, tenderness, lacerations and swelling. I check distal pulse, sensation and movement in the hand before touching the splint. While my partner maintains manual stabilization to minimize movement, I stabilize the bones and joints above and below the injury—the elbow and wrist—place a rigid splint under or alongside the forearm, pad between the limb and splint for even pressure and place the hand in the position of function with a roll of gauze in the palm, then secure it. I reassess distal pulse, sensation and movement and apply a sling, cold pack and elevation. A serious head injury is suggested by altered mental status, vomiting, unequal pupils, seizures, raccoon eyes, Battle’s sign or clear fluid from the nose or ears, and increased intracranial pressure appears as Cushing’s triad—rising blood pressure, slowing pulse and irregular respirations. I maintain spinal motion restriction, manage the airway and give oxygen to keep SpO₂ at least 94%, ventilating at a normal rate rather than hyperventilating. I track the Glasgow Coma Scale and pupils every 5 minutes and transport rapidly to a trauma center.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_FRACT,CDC_TBI]};

export const E8={title:'Medical emergencies, epinephrine and naloxone (Skills VIII and IX)',hook:'Medical calls outnumber trauma calls for most EMS agencies: shortness of breath, strokes, seizures, diabetic problems, allergic reactions and overdoses. EMTs can give a short list of medications that save lives. Skills VIII and IX test the epinephrine auto-injector and naloxone nasal spray, including documentation on the EMS medication report.',objectives:['Assess and treat respiratory emergencies, stroke, seizures and diabetic emergencies.','Recognize anaphylaxis and give epinephrine following HOSA Skill VIII.','Recognize opioid overdose and give naloxone following HOSA Skill IX.','Apply the rights of medication administration and document medications.'],sections:[
['Respiratory emergencies',[
'**Asthma** narrows the lower airways (wheezing, prolonged exhalation); **COPD** (emphysema and chronic bronchitis) causes chronic shortness of breath, often with barrel chest and pursed-lip breathing; **pneumonia** causes fever, cough and crackles; **pulmonary edema** from heart failure causes crackles and frothy sputum; a **pulmonary embolism** causes sudden shortness of breath and chest pain. Signs of distress include **tripod position**, accessory muscle use, inability to speak full sentences and cyanosis.',
'EMT care: position of comfort (usually sitting up), oxygen to keep SpO₂ **94% or higher** (COPD patients may target 88–92% per protocol), and assisting with the patient’s prescribed **metered-dose inhaler** or giving a nebulized bronchodilator if protocols allow. Assist ventilations with a BVM if breathing becomes inadequate. **CPAP** may be used by EMTs in some systems for pulmonary edema.',
match('Condition and clue',[['Asthma','Wheezing and prolonged exhalation'],['COPD','Barrel chest, pursed-lip breathing'],['Pneumonia','Fever, cough, crackles'],['Pulmonary edema','Crackles, frothy sputum, JVD'],['Pulmonary embolism','Sudden shortness of breath after immobility'],['Tripod position','Severe respiratory distress']])]],
['Stroke, seizures and diabetic emergencies',[
'**Stroke**: use a scale such as the **Cincinnati Prehospital Stroke Scale**—**facial droop**, **arm drift** and **abnormal speech**—or BE FAST. Determine the **last known well** time, check blood glucose (low sugar mimics stroke), give oxygen only if SpO₂ is below 94% and transport rapidly to a **stroke center**. **Seizures**: protect the patient, do not restrain or put anything in the mouth, manage the airway afterward (**postictal** confusion is normal) and treat **status epilepticus** (a seizure longer than 5 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery) as a life threat.',
'**Hypoglycemia** (glucose below about **70 mg/dL**) develops quickly: confusion, sweating, pale skin, tachycardia and seizures. If the patient is **awake and can swallow**, give **oral glucose** (about 15 g) between the cheek and gum. **Hyperglycemia** develops slowly; **diabetic ketoacidosis** causes deep, rapid **Kussmaul respirations**, fruity breath, thirst and dehydration. Unresponsive diabetic patients get airway care and rapid transport for IV dextrose.',
{t:'w',kind:'glucose'},
sort('Hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia/DKA?',['Hypoglycemia','Hyperglycemia/DKA'],[['Rapid onset with sweating and confusion',0],['Fruity breath and deep, rapid breathing',1],['Glucose 52 mg/dL',0],['Gradual onset with thirst and frequent urination',1],['Pale, clammy skin and shakiness',0]])]],
['Medication administration and anaphylaxis',[
'Before giving any medication, confirm the **rights**: right **patient**, **medication**, **dose**, **route**, **time** and **documentation**—plus checking the **expiration date** and **indications and contraindications**, and getting medical direction as protocols require. Record each dose on the **EMS medication report**: **medication, time, dosage, route, staff ID and response**.',
'**Anaphylaxis** is a life-threatening allergic reaction involving two or more body systems—skin (hives, flushing, swelling), respiratory (wheezing, stridor, throat tightness), cardiovascular (hypotension) or GI (vomiting, cramps)—after exposure to an allergen. **Epinephrine** constricts blood vessels and opens the airways; the adult auto-injector dose is **0.3 mg** and the pediatric dose **0.15 mg** (intramuscular). Side effects include rapid heart rate, anxiety, tremors and pale skin. Reassess and consider a second dose per protocol.',
flip('Medication rights and report',[['Right patient','Confirm the medication is prescribed to them'],['Right medication','Read the label'],['Right dose','Adult EpiPen 0.3 mg; pediatric 0.15 mg'],['Right route','Intramuscular, outer thigh'],['Right time','Record when it was given'],['Right documentation','Medication, time, dose, route, staff ID, response']])]],
['Epinephrine auto-injector: Skill VIII',[
'**1–4.** Scene safety, PPE, check responsiveness and breathing, introduce yourself and get permission. **5.** Ask what happened (the judge states the patient was exposed to an allergen and has an epinephrine pen). **6.** Look for **medical information jewelry**. **7.** **Obtain the EpiPen from the patient**. **8.** **Verify the medication and expiration date**. **9.** **Read the instructions** to find how long to hold the injector. **10.** Hold it in a **fist**. **11.** **Remove the safety cap**. **12.** **Hold the leg firmly**. **13.** Press the tip **hard against the side of the thigh, about halfway between the hip and knee**.',
'**14.** **Hold** for the manufacturer’s recommended time. **15.** **Pull straight out**, not touching the end that was pressed to the skin. **16.** Have the patient **rub the site for 10 seconds** (or rub it yourself). **17.** **Verbalize the time** of injection. **18.** **Assess any change** in status (the judge states improvement or worsening). **19.** **Dispose of the auto-injector properly** (sharps container). **20–24.** Communicate, report to the ED including the **time of injection**, BSI, PPE in a biohazard receptacle and hand rub.',
order('Skill VIII core steps',['Obtain the EpiPen; verify medication and expiration','Read the hold time','Fist grip; remove the safety cap','Hold the leg firmly','Press hard on the side of the thigh halfway between hip and knee','Hold, pull straight out, rub 10 seconds','Verbalize the time; reassess; dispose in sharps'])]],
['Naloxone nasal spray: Skill IX',[
'**1–3.** Scene safety, PPE, check responsiveness and breathing (the judge states agonal respirations or unresponsiveness). **4.** Quickly assess the situation—ask others what happened—and note **choking or gurgling** sounds. The judge states there is evidence of **opioid** use. **5.** Verbalize signs of overdose: **altered consciousness or drowsiness**, **choking or gurgling**, **small, constricted (pinpoint) pupils** and **blue skin, lips or nails**.',
'**6.** Administer naloxone: **a.** **inspect the nostril** for obstruction; **b.** peel back the package and **check the name and expiration date**; **c.** hold the device with your **thumb on the bottom of the plunger** and **two fingers on either side of the nozzle**; **d.** insert the tip into either nostril **until your fingers touch the bottom of the nose**; **e.** **press the plunger firmly**. **7.** **Record the name, dose, route and time**. **8.** **Continue to assess responsiveness and breathing**; if the status changes, begin **rescue breathing (1 breath every 6 seconds) or CPR**. Naloxone may cause abrupt **withdrawal** (agitation, vomiting), and opioid effects can return when it wears off.',
kase('Unresponsive in a restroom',{a:{text:'A young adult is found unresponsive with gurgling, pinpoint pupils and blue lips; a syringe is nearby. (Scenario invented for practice.) After scene safety and PPE, what do you do?',choices:[['Open the airway, start BVM ventilation and give naloxone nasal spray','b','Correct. Support breathing and reverse the opioid.',true],['Wait for ALS to arrive','b','Act now.',false]]},b:{text:'You gave naloxone at 14:05. What must be recorded?',choices:[['Name, dose, route and time','c','Correct.',true],['Only that a spray was given','c','Document fully.',false]]},c:{text:'Breathing improves. You continue to monitor and watch for withdrawal and re-sedation.',end:true}})]],
["Poisoning and behavioral emergencies",["Poisons enter the body by **ingestion**, **inhalation**, **injection** and **absorption**. Find out **what**, **how much**, **when** and **by what route**, bring containers to the hospital and contact **Poison Control (1-800-222-1222)** or medical direction. **Carbon monoxide** poisoning causes headache, nausea and confusion in several people at once; remove everyone from the source and give high-flow oxygen (pulse oximetry can be falsely normal). Some protocols allow **activated charcoal** for certain ingestions.","**Behavioral emergencies** include suicidal thoughts, psychosis, agitation and **excited delirium**. Ensure scene safety and law-enforcement support, speak calmly, avoid confrontation and look for medical causes such as hypoglycemia, head injury, hypoxia or drug use. Ask directly about suicidal thoughts; patients at risk should not be left alone.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Poisoning route and example","pairs":[["Ingestion","Swallowed medications or cleaners"],["Inhalation","Carbon monoxide"],["Injection","Drugs or venom"],["Absorption","Pesticide on the skin"],["Several people with headaches in one home","Suspect carbon monoxide"],["Agitated, confused patient","Check glucose and look for medical causes"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Respiratory: position of comfort, oxygen to 94% or more (COPD often 88–92% per protocol), assist with prescribed inhaler; stroke: facial droop, arm drift, speech, last known well, check glucose; status epilepticus is a seizure over 5 minutes.','Hypoglycemia below about 70 mg/dL develops fast—oral glucose if able to swallow; DKA causes Kussmaul breathing and fruity breath; medication rights: patient, medication, dose, route, time, documentation plus expiration.','Skill VIII: verify medication and expiration, read hold time, fist, cap off, hold leg, side of thigh halfway between hip and knee, hold, pull straight out, rub 10 seconds, time, reassess, sharps; Skill IX: pinpoint pupils, gurgling, blue lips—check nostril and expiration, thumb on plunger, fingers touch nose, press, record, rescue breaths or CPR.'],terms:[['Anaphylaxis','A severe allergic reaction involving more than one body system.'],['Status epilepticus','A seizure lasting more than 5 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery.'],['Kussmaul respirations','Deep, rapid breathing seen in diabetic ketoacidosis.']],mcqs:[q('Where is the EpiPen injected per Skill VIII?','Side of the thigh halfway between the hip and knee','Upper arm','Abdomen','Calf','Intramuscular.'),q('What is the adult epinephrine auto-injector dose?','0.3 mg','0.15 mg','3 mg','30 mg','Pediatric 0.15 mg.'),q('Which sign suggests opioid overdose?','Pinpoint pupils','Dilated pupils','Hyperactivity','High fever','Plus gurgling and blue lips.'),q('How is naloxone nasal spray held?','Thumb on the bottom of the plunger, two fingers beside the nozzle','By the nozzle only','Upside down','With the whole fist','Insert until fingers touch the nose.'),q('What is recorded after naloxone?','Name, dose, route and time','Only the time','Nothing','The patient’s insurance','Documentation.'),q('Which stroke scale checks facial droop, arm drift and speech?','Cincinnati Prehospital Stroke Scale','APGAR','Glasgow Coma Scale only','Rule of nines','Note last known well.'),q('Hypoglycemia in a patient who can swallow is treated with:','Oral glucose','Insulin','Nothing','Naloxone','About 15 g.'),q('Deep, rapid breathing with fruity breath suggests:','Diabetic ketoacidosis','Hypoglycemia','Stroke','Asthma','Kussmaul respirations.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how an EMT gives an epinephrine auto-injector following HOSA Skill VIII and naloxone nasal spray following HOSA Skill IX, including assessment and documentation.',rubric:[['Anaphylaxis assessment',['allergen','hives','swelling','wheezing','breathing','medical jewelry','permission']],['Epinephrine steps',['verify','expiration','instructions','fist','safety cap','leg','thigh','hip','knee','hold','straight out','rub','10 seconds','time','sharps']],['Overdose assessment',['agonal','unresponsive','gurgling','pinpoint','constricted','blue','opioid']],['Naloxone and documentation',['nostril','expiration','thumb','plunger','nozzle','press','name','dose','route','time','rescue breathing','cpr']]],model:'For anaphylaxis, after scene safety, PPE and checking responsiveness and breathing, I introduce myself, get permission and ask what happened, learning the patient was exposed to an allergen and has hives, swelling and wheezing. I look for medical information jewelry, obtain the patient’s EpiPen and verify the medication and expiration date, then read the instructions for the hold time. Holding it in my fist, I remove the safety cap, hold the leg firmly and press the tip hard against the side of the thigh halfway between the hip and knee, hold it for the recommended time and pull it straight out without touching the needle end. The site is rubbed for 10 seconds, I verbalize the time, reassess the patient and dispose of the injector in a sharps container, reporting the time of injection to the emergency department. For a suspected opioid overdose, I check responsiveness and breathing and find agonal respirations, gurgling, pinpoint pupils and blue lips with evidence of opioid use. I inspect the nostril for obstruction, check the naloxone name and expiration date, hold the device with my thumb on the bottom of the plunger and two fingers beside the nozzle, insert the tip until my fingers touch the bottom of the nose and press the plunger firmly. I record the name, dose, route and time and keep assessing responsiveness and breathing, giving rescue breaths every 6 seconds or CPR if the status changes.'},sources:[RULES,AAAAI_ANA,SAMHSA_NAL,STROKE]};

export const E9={title:'Pediatrics and childbirth',hook:'Children are not small adults. Their airways are narrower, they compensate for shock until they suddenly crash and they cannot always describe what hurts. Childbirth is usually normal, but EMTs must be ready to deliver, resuscitate a newborn and handle complications. Pediatrics and childbirth together make up 16% of the EMT test.',objectives:['Use the pediatric assessment triangle and age-appropriate vital signs.','Recognize pediatric respiratory distress, shock and common emergencies.','Assist with a normal delivery and assess the newborn with the APGAR score.','Manage childbirth complications and newborn resuscitation.'],sections:[
['The pediatric assessment triangle and vital signs',[
'The **pediatric assessment triangle (PAT)** forms a general impression from the doorway: **appearance** (tone, interactiveness, consolability, look or gaze, speech or cry—**TICLS**), **work of breathing** (abnormal sounds, retractions, nasal flaring, head bobbing, tripod position) and **circulation to the skin** (pallor, mottling, cyanosis). An abnormal side means the child is sick.',
'Normal vital signs change with age: **newborns** have a heart rate around **100–160** and respirations **30–60**; **toddlers** about **90–150** and **24–40**; **school-age children** about **70–120** and **18–30**. A child aged 1–10 is **hypotensive** if systolic BP is **below 70 + (2 × age in years)**. **Capillary refill** longer than 2 seconds suggests poor perfusion. Use a length-based tape to estimate size and equipment.',
flip('Pediatric assessment triangle',[['Appearance','Tone, interactiveness, consolability, gaze, cry (TICLS)'],['Work of breathing','Retractions, flaring, head bobbing, sounds'],['Circulation to skin','Pallor, mottling, cyanosis'],['Hypotension, age 1–10','SBP below 70 + 2 × age'],['Capillary refill','Normal under 2 seconds'],['Infant heart rate','About 100–160']])]],
['Pediatric emergencies',[
'**Respiratory problems** cause most pediatric arrests. **Croup** (viral, barking cough, stridor), **epiglottitis** (high fever, drooling, tripod position—do not look in the throat), **asthma**, **bronchiolitis** (RSV in infants) and **foreign body airway obstruction** are common. Keep the child calm and with the caregiver, give oxygen without frightening them (blow-by if needed) and ventilate with a BVM at **1 breath every 2–3 seconds** if breathing is inadequate.',
'**Febrile seizures** occur with rapid fever in children 6 months to 6 years; they are usually brief but still need evaluation. **Dehydration** from vomiting and diarrhea causes sunken eyes and fontanelle, dry mouth and few wet diapers. **Sudden unexpected infant death (SUID)**—promote **safe sleep**: alone, on the back, in a crib. **Child abuse** signs include injuries inconsistent with the history, multiple bruises in different stages of healing and delays in seeking care; EMTs are **mandated reporters**.',
match('Pediatric condition and clue',[['Croup','Barking cough and stridor'],['Epiglottitis','Drooling, high fever, tripod position'],['Bronchiolitis','Wheezing infant with RSV'],['Febrile seizure','Seizure with rapid fever, 6 months–6 years'],['Dehydration','Sunken fontanelle, few wet diapers'],['Possible abuse','Injury inconsistent with the story']])]],
['Labor and a normal delivery',[
'Labor has **three stages**: **first**—from contractions to **full cervical dilation**; **second**—from full dilation to **delivery of the baby**; **third**—delivery of the **placenta**. Signs of **imminent delivery** include **crowning** (the head visible at the vaginal opening), contractions about **2 minutes apart**, the urge to push or have a bowel movement, and the mother saying the baby is coming—prepare to deliver on scene. Ask about the due date, number of pregnancies, prenatal care and whether the water has broken (note any **meconium**).',
'Use full PPE and an OB kit. Support the head as it emerges and **check for a nuchal cord** (cord around the neck)—slip it over the head if loose. Support the baby as the shoulders deliver, **dry, warm and stimulate** the newborn, suction the mouth then the nose **only if needed**, and keep the baby level with the mother. Clamp the cord in two places several inches from the baby and cut between the clamps after it stops pulsing per protocol. Deliver the placenta and bring it to the hospital; control bleeding with **fundal massage** (up to about 500 mL of blood loss is common).',
order('Normal delivery',['Prepare: PPE and OB kit','Support the head as it crowns','Check for a nuchal cord','Support the shoulders and body','Dry, warm and stimulate the newborn','Clamp and cut the cord','Deliver the placenta; fundal massage'])]],
['Newborn assessment and resuscitation',[
'The **APGAR** score is measured at **1 and 5 minutes**: **A**ppearance (skin color), **P**ulse (heart rate), **G**rimace (reflex irritability), **A**ctivity (muscle tone) and **R**espiration—each scored 0–2, for a total of **0–10**. A score of 7–10 is reassuring; 4–6 needs help; 0–3 needs resuscitation. Do not delay resuscitation to calculate the APGAR.',
'**Newborn resuscitation:** warm, dry and stimulate (rub the back, flick the soles). If the newborn is **not breathing or the heart rate is below 100**, give **BVM ventilation** at about **40–60 breaths per minute**. If the heart rate stays **below 60** despite effective ventilation for 30 seconds, begin **chest compressions** with the two-thumb technique at a **3:1 ratio** (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute).',
{t:'table',caption:'APGAR score',head:['Sign','0','1','2'],rows:[['Appearance','Blue or pale all over','Body pink, extremities blue','Pink all over'],['Pulse','Absent','Below 100','100 or more'],['Grimace','No response','Grimace','Cry or pull away'],['Activity','Limp','Some flexion','Active motion'],['Respiration','Absent','Slow, irregular','Good, crying']]}]],
['Childbirth complications',[
'**Breech** (buttocks or feet first): support the body and, if the head does not deliver, place a gloved hand to create an **airway** for the baby. **Prolapsed cord** (cord comes out first): place the mother in a **knee-chest** or Trendelenburg position, use gloved fingers to **lift the presenting part off the cord**, cover the cord with moist dressings and transport rapidly. **Limb presentation**: transport immediately. **Postpartum hemorrhage** (more than about 500 mL): fundal massage, treat shock and transport.',
'Other emergencies include **preeclampsia/eclampsia** (high blood pressure, headache, visual changes, seizures), **placenta previa** (painless bright red bleeding) and **abruptio placentae** (painful bleeding and a rigid abdomen). Late in pregnancy, the uterus can compress the vena cava when the mother lies flat (**supine hypotensive syndrome**); transport her on her **left side**.',
kase('Delivery on scene',{a:{text:'A woman is in labor with contractions 2 minutes apart, and the head is crowning. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Prepare to deliver on scene with PPE and an OB kit','b','Correct. Delivery is imminent.',true],['Load her and drive fast to the hospital','b','Delivery is imminent.',false]]},b:{text:'The baby is limp, blue and not breathing after drying and stimulation. What next?',choices:[['Begin BVM ventilation','c','Correct. Heart rate check follows.',true],['Calculate the APGAR first','c','Do not delay resuscitation.',false]]},c:{text:'After 30 seconds of ventilation, the heart rate is 120 and the baby cries. You keep the newborn warm.',end:true}})]],
["Children with special health care needs",["Some children depend on medical technology: **tracheostomy tubes** (can block with mucus—suction and, if trained, replace), **home ventilators** (if the ventilator fails, disconnect and ventilate with a BVM), **gastrostomy (feeding) tubes**, **central IV lines** and **shunts** for hydrocephalus (signs of a blocked shunt include headache, vomiting and altered mental status). Parents and caregivers are the experts on their child’s condition and equipment.","Many families keep an **emergency information form** listing diagnoses, medications, allergies and baseline vital signs. Ask for it, bring the child’s equipment and supplies and transport to a hospital familiar with the child when possible. Speak to the child at eye level and keep them with their caregiver whenever it is safe.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Device and problem","pairs":[["Tracheostomy tube","Mucus plug—suction"],["Home ventilator failure","Disconnect and use a BVM"],["Shunt malfunction","Headache, vomiting, altered mental status"],["Feeding tube","Can dislodge or block"],["Central IV line","Infection and bleeding risk"],["Emergency information form","Diagnoses, medications and baseline vitals"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Pediatric assessment triangle: appearance (TICLS), work of breathing, circulation to skin; hypotension age 1–10 is SBP below 70 + 2 × age; respiratory problems cause most pediatric arrests; ventilate children every 2–3 seconds.','Labor stages: dilation, delivery of the baby, placenta; crowning means imminent delivery; check for a nuchal cord; dry, warm, stimulate; clamp and cut the cord; fundal massage for bleeding.','APGAR at 1 and 5 minutes (appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, respiration); ventilate if apneic or HR below 100; compressions 3:1 if HR below 60; prolapsed cord: knee-chest and lift the presenting part; left side for supine hypotension.'],terms:[['Pediatric assessment triangle','A quick look at appearance, work of breathing and circulation to the skin.'],['Crowning','The baby’s head visible at the vaginal opening.'],['APGAR score','A 0–10 newborn assessment at 1 and 5 minutes.']],mcqs:[q('Which is part of the pediatric assessment triangle?','Work of breathing','Blood glucose','Temperature','Blood pressure only','Plus appearance and circulation to skin.'),q('A 4-year-old is hypotensive if systolic BP is below:','78 mm Hg','100 mm Hg','60 mm Hg','120 mm Hg','70 + 2 × 4.'),q('A barking cough and stridor in a toddler suggest:','Croup','Epiglottitis','Asthma','Pneumonia only','Viral.'),q('Which sign indicates imminent delivery?','Crowning','Mild back pain','Nausea','Contractions 20 minutes apart','Prepare to deliver.'),q('When is the APGAR score measured?','At 1 and 5 minutes','At 10 and 20 minutes','Only at the hospital','Before birth','Five signs.'),q('Newborn chest compressions begin when the heart rate is below:','60 despite ventilation','100','140','160','3:1 ratio.'),q('For a prolapsed cord, the EMT should:','Place the mother knee-chest and lift the presenting part off the cord','Push the cord back in','Pull the cord','Have the mother walk','Transport rapidly.'),q('A late-pregnancy patient is transported on her left side to prevent:','Supine hypotensive syndrome','Eclampsia','Breech birth','Croup','Uterus compresses the vena cava.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how an EMT assists with a normal field delivery and assesses and resuscitates the newborn, and explain how to manage a prolapsed cord.',rubric:[['Preparation and delivery',['crowning','imminent','ppe','ob kit','support the head','nuchal cord','shoulders']],['Newborn care',['dry','warm','stimulate','suction','if needed','clamp','cut','cord','placenta','fundal massage']],['Assessment and resuscitation',['apgar','1 and 5 minutes','appearance','pulse','grimace','activity','respiration','ventilation','100','60','3:1']],['Prolapsed cord',['knee-chest','trendelenburg','lift','presenting part','moist','transport']]],model:'When contractions are about 2 minutes apart and the head is crowning, delivery is imminent, so I prepare to deliver on scene with full PPE and an OB kit. I support the baby’s head as it emerges, check for a nuchal cord and slip it over the head if it is loose, and support the shoulders and body as they deliver. I immediately dry, warm and stimulate the newborn, suctioning the mouth and then the nose only if secretions block the airway, and keep the baby level with the mother. I clamp the cord in two places several inches from the baby and cut between the clamps, then deliver the placenta, bring it to the hospital and use fundal massage to control bleeding. I calculate the APGAR score at 1 and 5 minutes—appearance, pulse, grimace, activity and respiration, each scored 0–2—but I do not delay resuscitation to do it. If the newborn is not breathing or the heart rate is below 100, I ventilate with a BVM at about 40–60 breaths per minute, and if the heart rate stays below 60 after 30 seconds of effective ventilation, I begin two-thumb chest compressions at a 3:1 ratio. For a prolapsed cord, I place the mother in a knee-chest or Trendelenburg position, use gloved fingers to lift the presenting part off the cord, cover the cord with moist sterile dressings, give oxygen and transport immediately.'},sources:[RULES,APGAR,SAFE_SLEEP,NREMT]};

export const E10={title:'Environmental emergencies and special situations',hook:'Heat, cold, water, altitude, lightning and venom can all injure or kill, and EMTs often reach these patients in difficult places. EMS operations—triage at mass-casualty incidents, hazardous materials and the incident command system—keep responders safe when calls get big. Environmental emergencies are 10% of the test and special situations 4%.',objectives:['Assess and treat heat and cold emergencies.','Care for drowning, diving, altitude, lightning and bite or sting injuries.','Triage patients at a mass-casualty incident using START.','Operate safely at hazardous materials incidents within the incident command system.'],sections:[
['Heat emergencies',[
'The body loses heat by **radiation**, **convection**, **conduction**, **evaporation** and **respiration**. **Heat cramps** are painful muscle spasms; **heat exhaustion** causes heavy sweating, cool, clammy skin, weakness, dizziness, nausea and a normal or slightly raised temperature; **heat stroke** is a life threat with **altered mental status**, very high body temperature (often above **104 °F/40 °C**) and hot skin that may be dry or moist. Exertional heat stroke is common in athletes and laborers.',
'Care: move the patient to a cool place and remove excess clothing. For heat exhaustion, give oxygen if needed, have the patient lie down with legs elevated and give fluids by mouth if fully alert. For heat stroke, **cool aggressively**—**cold-water immersion** is best; otherwise, cold packs to the neck, armpits and groin, wet sheets and fanning—give oxygen, manage the airway and transport rapidly. Older adults, infants and people taking certain medications are at higher risk.',
sort('Heat exhaustion or heat stroke?',['Heat exhaustion','Heat stroke'],[['Heavy sweating with normal mental status',0],['Temperature 106 °F and confusion',1],['Dizziness and nausea after yard work',0],['Seizure during a marathon',1],['Cool, clammy skin',0]])]],
['Cold emergencies',[
'**Hypothermia** is a core temperature below **95 °F (35 °C)**. **Mild**: shivering, rapid breathing, clumsiness; **moderate**: confusion, slurred speech, shivering may stop; **severe**: slow pulse and breathing, unresponsiveness and dangerous heart rhythms such as ventricular fibrillation. Handle patients **gently** (rough movement can trigger VF), remove wet clothing, insulate, give warm humidified oxygen if available and apply heat packs to the trunk. Check the pulse for up to **30–45 seconds** in severe hypothermia; patients are “**not dead until warm and dead**.”',
'**Frostbite** (deep tissue freezing) leaves skin white, waxy and hard; **frostnip** is superficial and reversible. Do not rub or rewarm if refreezing is possible; protect the area with dry, sterile dressings and transport. **Immersion foot (trench foot)** follows prolonged exposure to cold, wet conditions.',
match('Cold injury and care',[['Mild hypothermia','Remove wet clothing, insulate, warm'],['Severe hypothermia','Handle gently; long pulse check'],['Frostbite','Do not rub; protect with dry dressings'],['Frostnip','Superficial; rewarm gently'],['Immersion foot','Prolonged cold, wet exposure'],['Hypothermic arrest','Not dead until warm and dead']])]],
['Water, altitude, lightning, bites and stings',[
'**Drowning**: ensure your safety (reach, throw, row, then go only if trained), support the spine if diving is suspected, and focus on **ventilation and oxygen**—hypoxia causes the arrest. **Diving emergencies**: **decompression sickness (“the bends”)**—joint pain, rash, paralysis after ascent—and **arterial gas embolism**, which can cause stroke-like signs; give high-flow oxygen and transport to a facility with **hyperbaric** capability. **Altitude illness** ranges from acute mountain sickness to high-altitude pulmonary and cerebral edema; descend and give oxygen.',
'**Lightning**: use **reverse triage**—treat apparently dead victims first, because many are in respiratory or cardiac arrest that can be reversed. **Snakebites**: keep the patient calm, remove jewelry, keep the limb at heart level and transport; do not cut, suck or apply ice or a tourniquet. **Black widow** bites cause severe muscle cramps; **brown recluse** bites cause tissue necrosis. **Stings** can cause anaphylaxis—scrape away the stinger and be ready with epinephrine.',
match('Environmental emergency and key care',[['Drowning','Ventilation and oxygen'],['Decompression sickness','High-flow oxygen; hyperbaric center'],['Altitude illness','Descend and give oxygen'],['Multiple lightning victims','Reverse triage'],['Snakebite','Calm, still, limb at heart level'],['Bee sting with hives and wheezing','Epinephrine']])]],
['Mass-casualty incidents and START triage',[
'A **mass-casualty incident (MCI)** has more patients than responders can manage with normal resources. **START** (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) sorts adults in about **60 seconds** each: anyone who can **walk** is **green (minor)**. For the rest, check **respirations**: if not breathing after repositioning the airway, **black (deceased/expectant)**; if breathing **over 30** per minute, **red (immediate)**. Check **perfusion**: **capillary refill over 2 seconds** or **no radial pulse** = **red**. Check **mental status**: **cannot follow simple commands** = **red**. Otherwise **yellow (delayed)**.',
'The only treatments during START are **opening the airway** and **controlling major bleeding**. **JumpSTART** modifies START for children: if a child is not breathing, check for a pulse and give **five rescue breaths**; respirations below **15 or above 45** are red. Patients are moved to **treatment areas** by color and transported in priority order.',
sort('START category',['Red—immediate','Yellow—delayed','Green—minor'],[['Walking with a cut arm',2],['Breathing 36 per minute',0],['No radial pulse',0],['Cannot walk, breathing 18, follows commands, radial pulse present',1],['Cannot follow simple commands',0]])]],
['Hazardous materials and incident command',[
'At a **hazmat** scene, stay **upwind, uphill and upstream**, use binoculars to read **placards and labels** and look up the material in the **Emergency Response Guidebook (ERG)**. Scenes are divided into the **hot zone** (contamination; entered only by trained hazmat teams), the **warm zone** (decontamination corridor) and the **cold zone** (staging and treatment, where EMTs work). Patients must be **decontaminated** before EMS care.',
'The **National Incident Management System (NIMS)** and the **Incident Command System (ICS)** organize large responses under one **incident commander**, with sections for **operations**, **planning**, **logistics** and **finance/administration**. EMS branches include triage, treatment, transport and staging. **Ambulance operations** include vehicle inspection, safe driving with lights and sirens only when needed, scene positioning and cleaning after every call. Special patients—**geriatric**, **bariatric** and those with **disabilities or technology dependence** (ventilators, tracheostomies, feeding tubes)—need adapted assessment and equipment.',
kase('Bus crash',{a:{text:'You arrive first at a bus crash with about 25 patients. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is your first action?',choices:[['Establish command, call for more resources and begin triage','b','Correct.',true],['Start full treatment on the first patient you see','b','Organize first.',false]]},b:{text:'A patient is not breathing; after you reposition the airway, they still are not breathing. Category?',choices:[['Black (deceased/expectant)','c','Correct under START.',true],['Red (immediate)','c','Breathing after repositioning would be red.',false]]},c:{text:'You continue triage, sending walking patients to the green area and reporting counts to command.',end:true}})]],
["Responder safety on the road and at violent scenes",["Traffic crashes and struck-by incidents are major causes of EMS line-of-duty injury. Wear a **high-visibility vest** at roadway scenes, park the ambulance to **protect the scene** with emergency lights and work on the side away from traffic. Use **lights and sirens** only when the patient’s condition justifies it, wear seat belts in the patient compartment when possible and use car seats or approved restraints for children.","At **violent or crime scenes**, stage until law enforcement declares the scene safe, plan an escape route and avoid standing in doorways. Preserve evidence: touch only what you must, cut around bullet or knife holes in clothing and document what you saw and moved. In an **active shooter** event, EMS works in **warm zones** with law-enforcement protection (rescue task forces) to control bleeding quickly.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Safe responder practice?","buckets":["Safe","Unsafe"],"items":[["Wearing a high-visibility vest on the highway",0],["Entering a violent scene before police clear it",1],["Parking to block traffic around the crash",0],["Cutting through a bullet hole in clothing",1],["Staging until the scene is declared safe",0],["Using lights and sirens for every call",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Heat stroke has altered mental status and very high temperature—cool aggressively (cold-water immersion best); hypothermia below 95 °F: handle gently, remove wet clothing, long pulse check, not dead until warm and dead; frostbite: do not rub.','Drowning: ventilation and oxygen; decompression sickness and gas embolism: high-flow oxygen and hyperbaric care; lightning: reverse triage; snakebite: calm, still, limb at heart level, no cutting or ice.','START: walking = green; not breathing after repositioning = black; RR over 30, cap refill over 2 seconds or no radial pulse, or cannot follow commands = red; otherwise yellow; hazmat: upwind, uphill, ERG, hot, warm and cold zones; ICS under one incident commander.'],terms:[['START triage','A rapid triage system for mass-casualty incidents.'],['Reverse triage','Treating apparently dead lightning victims first.'],['Cold zone','The safe area outside a hazmat scene where EMS works.']],mcqs:[q('Which sign distinguishes heat stroke?','Altered mental status','Heavy sweating','Thirst','Muscle cramps','Plus very high temperature.'),q('The best cooling method for exertional heat stroke is:','Cold-water immersion','Warm blankets','Fluids only','Fanning alone','Cool aggressively.'),q('Severe hypothermia patients should be handled:','Gently','Roughly to warm them','By rubbing the limbs','Only after warming','Rough movement can trigger VF.'),q('Lightning strikes with multiple victims use:','Reverse triage','START only','No triage','Black tags first','Treat the apparently dead first.'),q('Under START, a patient breathing 34 times per minute is:','Red','Green','Yellow','Black','Over 30.'),q('Under START, a patient who can walk is:','Green','Red','Yellow','Black','Minor.'),q('At a hazmat scene, EMTs should stage:','Upwind and uphill','Downwind','In the hot zone','Downhill','Use the ERG.'),q('EMS patient care at a hazmat incident occurs in the:','Cold zone','Hot zone','Warm zone only','Exclusion zone','After decontamination.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how an EMT recognizes and treats heat stroke and severe hypothermia, and describe how START triage sorts patients at a mass-casualty incident.',rubric:[['Heat stroke',['altered mental status','104','hot skin','cool','cold-water immersion','ice packs','oxygen','transport']],['Hypothermia',['95','shivering','confusion','gently','wet clothing','insulate','pulse','30-45 seconds','warm and dead']],['START process',['walk','green','respirations','reposition','black','30','red','capillary refill','2 seconds','radial','commands','yellow']],['MCI operations',['command','resources','airway','bleeding','treatment area','transport']]],model:'Heat stroke is recognized by altered mental status—confusion, seizures or unresponsiveness—together with a very high body temperature, often above 104 °F, and hot skin. I move the patient to a cool area, remove clothing and cool aggressively, preferably by cold-water immersion or otherwise with ice packs to the neck, armpits and groin, wet sheets and fanning, while giving oxygen, managing the airway and transporting rapidly. Severe hypothermia, a core temperature well below 95 °F, causes confusion that progresses to unresponsiveness, and shivering may stop; I handle the patient gently because rough movement can trigger ventricular fibrillation, remove wet clothing, insulate and warm the trunk, check the pulse for 30–45 seconds and remember that patients are not dead until they are warm and dead. At a mass-casualty incident, I establish command and request more resources, then use START: everyone who can walk is tagged green. For the rest, I check breathing—if the patient is not breathing after the airway is repositioned, they are black; if breathing more than 30 times per minute, they are red. I check perfusion—capillary refill over 2 seconds or no radial pulse is red—and mental status—inability to follow simple commands is red. Everyone else is yellow. The only treatments during triage are opening the airway and controlling major bleeding, and patients then move to treatment areas and are transported in priority order.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_HEAT,START,NIMS,ERG]};
